{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789548730,"nonce":"Ft5iIXyYwb2FZuqbHxGLki56Oyg=","title":"Comprehension target changes a later variable's type","message":"A comprehension target must be local to the comprehension. In zypy it shares a compiler name with a later loop variable, so the later variable can be treated as an integer address instead of its list value.\n\n    def f(pieces):\n        grid = [[[] for p in range(len(pieces))] for row in range(1)]\n        for _, p in enumerate(pieces):\n            print(p)\n    f([[1, 2], [3, 4]])\n\nCPython prints `[1, 2]` and `[3, 4]`; before the fix zypy prints pointer-sized integers. Renaming the comprehension's `p` to `q` makes zypy print the lists.\n\nThis caused a signal 11 in the unmodified meteor-contest kernel while `get_footprints()` iterated `p`. The target of a list, set or dict comprehension needs a distinct internal name, and its scope should still read enclosing variables without binding its target there.","files":null}]}